LeetCode 1875 — Group Employees of the Same Salary (Medium):

Table: Employees
+-------------+---------+
| Column Name | Type    |
+-------------+---------+
| employee_id | int     |
| name        | varchar |
| salary      | int     |
+-------------+---------+
employee_id is the primary key for this table. Each row of this table indicates the employee ID, employee name, and salary. GitHub

Task
A company wants to divide the employees into teams such that all the members on each team have the same salary. The teams should follow these criteria: GitHub

Each team should consist of at least two employees. GitHub
All the employees on a team should have the same salary. GitHub
All the employees of the same salary should be assigned to the same team. GitHub
If the salary of an employee is unique, we do not assign this employee to any team. GitHub
A team's ID is assigned based on the rank of the team's salary relative to the other teams' salaries, where the team with the lowest salary has team_id = 1. Note that the salaries for employees not on a team are not included in this ranking. GitHub

Write an SQL query to get the team_id of each employee that is in a team. Return the result table ordered by team_id in ascending order. In case of a tie, order it by employee_id in ascending order. GitHub
The query result format is in the following example.

Example
Input — Employees table:
+-------------+---------+--------+
| employee_id | name    | salary |
+-------------+---------+--------+
| 2           | Meir    | 3000   |
| 3           | Michael | 3000   |
| 7           | Addilyn | 7400   |
| 8           | Juan    | 6100   |
| 9           | Kannon  | 7400   |
+-------------+---------+--------+
Output:
+-------------+---------+--------+---------+
| employee_id | name    | salary | team_id |
+-------------+---------+--------+---------+
| 2           | Meir    | 3000   | 1       |
| 3           | Michael | 3000   | 1       |
| 7           | Addilyn | 7400   | 2       |
| 9           | Kannon  | 7400   | 2       |
+-------------+---------+--------+---------+

Solution
--------
WITH valid_salary AS (
    SELECT
        salary
    FROM employees
    GROUP BY salary
    HAVING COUNT(salary) > 1
)

SELECT 
    e.*,
    DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY e.salary) AS team_id
FROM employees e 
WHERE e.salary IN (SELECT salary FROM valid_salary)
ORDER BY team_id, employee_id;
